          "text": "## Your sleep over the last 3 weeks (26 Feb – 18 Mar)\n\n**Before the numbers: most of this data doesn't look right.** For 20 of the 21 nights (26 Feb to 17 Mar), every reading except sleep length is exactly the same: sleep score 63, sleep stress 19, SpO₂ 94% (lowest 87%), overnight HRV 61 ms, 13 minutes awake over 2 wake-ups, and about the same stage split each night. Real nights don't repeat like that.\n\nThe timings don't add up either. On each of those nights, the recorded window from falling asleep to waking is about 23:45–05:40 Athens time, which is about 5.9 hours. But many of those nights report more sleep than that, up to 8.1 hours. **Last night (18 Mar) is the only one that's consistent.** Its numbers vary normally and the 6.25 h of sleep plus 26 min awake fits its 23:11–05:52 window.\n\nThis could be a sync problem, a watch that wasn't worn properly, or placeholder data. I can't tell which from here. It's worth opening the nightly sleep views in Garmin Connect to check. Treat everything below except last night with caution.\n\n### Sleep length, as reported\n| Week | Average sleep | Range |\n|---|---|---|\n| 26 Feb – 4 Mar | ~7.2 h | 6.1 – 8.1 h |\n| 5 – 11 Mar | ~7.0 h | 5.9 – 8.0 h |\n| 12 – 18 Mar | ~6.7 h | 5.7 – 7.6 h |\n\n- **Overall average:** about 7.0 h a night.\n- **Under 6 hours:** 2 nights, 7 Mar (5.9 h) and 17 Mar (5.7 h).\n- **Trend:** if these durations are right, you've been sleeping less lately. The last 3 nights were 6.2 h, 5.7 h and 6.25 h, below your 3-week average.\n- **No naps** were recorded.\n\n### Last night (18 Mar), the one night that looks reliable\n- **Sleep score:** 74 (Fair), higher than the flat 63 on the other nights.\n- **Sleep:** 6.25 h, with 26 min awake over 1 wake-up.\n- **Stages:** 1 h 10 min deep (18.7%), 3 h 45 min light (60%), 1 h 20 min REM (21.3%).\n- **Sleep stress:** 14. **Overnight HRV:** 58 ms. **SpO₂:** 95% average, 88% lowest.\n\n### Takeaways\n1. **The biggest issue is the data.** You can't read much into scores, HRV or stress over this stretch. They never change, so they hide any real night-to-night differences.\n2. **If the durations are accurate, you've been sleeping less this past week.** Three of the last four nights were under 6.5 h. Last night's 6.25 h is still on the short side.\n\nI can check your HRV trend or training readiness for the same period to see whether they look more trustworthy and show any effect from the shorter nights.\n\n*Training data only, not medical advice.*",
